1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,411
Can we trust a noisy quantum computer?

2
00:00:02,411 --> 00:00:03,572
We are team twelve.

3
00:00:03,572 --> 00:00:09,302
Our project studies this question through a small Bell-state experiment on real IBM hardware.

4
00:00:09,302 --> 00:00:13,502
The team members are Hung-Yu Lin, Xinchen Geng, and Daniel Cai.

5
00:00:13,502 --> 00:00:22,093
We investigate two practical choices: where to place the experiment on a processor, and when adding error detection is worth its cost.

6
00:00:22,093 --> 00:00:27,196
Our main finding is that a better physical placement helped in two new confirmation runs.

7
00:00:27,196 --> 00:00:34,610
Error detection helped a declared injected-error mixture, but did not establish a general improvement under natural noise.

8
00:00:34,610 --> 00:00:39,567
We prepare the Bell state Phi-plus with a Hadamard gate followed by a controlled NOT.

9
00:00:39,567 --> 00:00:45,349
We measure the two data qubits in three settings: X X, Y Y, and Z Z.

10
00:00:45,349 --> 00:00:49,049
These correlations estimate fidelity to the known target state.

11
00:00:49,049 --> 00:00:56,220
The expression adds the X X and Z Z correlations and subtracts Y Y, before adding one and dividing by four.

12
00:00:56,220 --> 00:01:04,279
Measuring only Z Z would be insufficient, because a classical mixture of zero zero and one one can show the same Z correlation.

13
00:01:04,279 --> 00:01:08,862
The three-setting test checks the coherence that this simple classical check misses.

14
00:01:08,862 --> 00:01:14,072
The project includes both ideal simulator counts and a synthetic noisy baseline.

15
00:01:14,072 --> 00:01:19,084
Its noise assumptions are recorded rather than presented as IBM calibration data.

16
00:01:19,084 --> 00:01:23,818
The protected circuit adds an auxiliary qubit that records odd Z Z parity.

17
00:01:23,818 --> 00:01:27,138
We keep the shots whose measured auxiliary bit is zero.

18
00:01:27,138 --> 00:01:33,427
This can detect a single X error occurring before the check, while a phase error can pass undetected.

19
00:01:33,427 --> 00:01:35,366
All measurements happen at the end.

20
00:01:35,366 --> 00:01:38,021
This is error detection with postselection.

21
00:01:38,021 --> 00:01:44,136
It does not implement general error correction, and each extra operation can itself introduce noise.

22
00:01:44,136 --> 00:01:48,426
Our initial hardware run illustrates why a protection method needs measurement.

23
00:01:48,426 --> 00:01:55,605
On the original placement, the ordinary circuit gave a raw fidelity estimate of 96 point 85 percent.

24
00:01:55,605 --> 00:02:02,893
The filtered circuit gave 95 point 13 percent and retained 97 point 36 percent of its shots.

25
00:02:02,893 --> 00:02:09,075
The ordinary preparation used one native controlled Z gate, while the routed detector used six.

26
00:02:09,075 --> 00:02:12,752
The added detector did not improve the measured result in this run.

27
00:02:12,752 --> 00:02:21,026
Its extra gates and routing are possible contributors, but this experiment does not isolate the cause or prove that detection always hurts.

28
00:02:21,026 --> 00:02:30,035
We next explored physical placement across all 176 unordered connected data-qubit pairs on the captured processor topology.

29
00:02:30,035 --> 00:02:36,811
This scan covered one prescribed orientation per new pair, rather than every possible circuit configuration.

30
00:02:36,811 --> 00:02:43,383
Screening maxima are exploratory because selecting the largest observed value can exaggerate performance.

31
00:02:43,383 --> 00:02:48,143
We froze a candidate and its controls before running two new confirmation jobs.

32
00:02:48,143 --> 00:02:52,865
Each candidate used 16,384 shots per basis.

33
00:02:52,865 --> 00:02:58,752
Both new jobs had to pass a fixed improvement criterion and a conservative adjusted difference interval.

34
00:02:58,752 --> 00:03:03,060
We preserved the exploration and the unsuccessful results for inspection.

35
00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:05,827
Here are the independent confirmation results.

36
00:03:05,827 --> 00:03:17,166
In confirmation A, the original configuration gave 95 point 81 percent fidelity, while the selected layout plus reset gave 97 point 63 percent.

37
00:03:17,166 --> 00:03:26,113
In confirmation B, the original gave 90 point 53 percent and the selected configuration gave 97 point 73 percent.

38
00:03:26,113 --> 00:03:27,601
All shots were retained.

39
00:03:27,601 --> 00:03:30,527
Both adjusted difference intervals stayed above zero.

40
00:03:30,527 --> 00:03:35,226
These results support a better-performing configuration among the tested choices.

41
00:03:35,226 --> 00:03:44,090
They do not establish a global optimum across all placements, orientations, pulse schedules, or future calibration conditions.

42
00:03:44,090 --> 00:03:46,316
The same-layout control is important.

43
00:03:46,316 --> 00:03:54,606
Without the extra reset, the selected layout gave 97 point 79 percent and 97 point 65 percent.

44
00:03:54,606 --> 00:04:00,501
The reset-specific differences were small, changed sign, and had intervals including zero.

45
00:04:00,501 --> 00:04:05,423
We therefore cannot attribute the selected configuration's benefit to reset itself.

46
00:04:05,423 --> 00:04:09,565
The original baseline also changed substantially between jobs.

47
00:04:09,565 --> 00:04:17,849
Our intervals describe finite-shot variation, not calibration drift, measurement bias, or every other systematic effect.

48
00:04:17,849 --> 00:04:25,032
The defensible claim is an observed configuration benefit in these confirmations, with its limits stated alongside it.

49
00:04:25,032 --> 00:04:29,804
We separately tested the mechanism of error detection on a fixed physical layout.

50
00:04:29,804 --> 00:04:36,406
Each setting used nine copies without an injected fault and one copy with an X fault after Bell preparation.

51
00:04:36,406 --> 00:04:42,490
We combined these counts before postselection, producing a declared ten percent X-error mixture.

52
00:04:42,490 --> 00:04:48,976
This is a fixed stratified allocation, rather than randomly deciding whether to add an error on each shot.

53
00:04:48,976 --> 00:04:53,117
Two new jobs compared the ordinary Bell circuit with the bridge detector.

54
00:04:53,117 --> 00:05:00,881
The no-injected-error comparison uses a subset of the same data and remains descriptive rather than a separate independent discovery.

55
00:05:00,881 --> 00:05:07,377
Under the declared ten percent X-error mixture, ordinary fidelity was about 88 point 2 percent.

56
00:05:07,377 --> 00:05:13,167
The bridge detector's retained-shot fidelity was about 97 point 3 percent in both jobs.

57
00:05:13,167 --> 00:05:17,638
Both primary ninety-nine percent bootstrap difference intervals were positive.

58
00:05:17,638 --> 00:05:22,976
The cost was that the detector discarded about eleven point one percent of attempted shots.

59
00:05:22,976 --> 00:05:26,773
Without injected errors, it did not establish an improvement.

60
00:05:26,773 --> 00:05:36,229
Higher quality among retained samples does not mean a higher unconditional output yield, and these results do not prove protection against arbitrary errors

61
00:05:36,229 --> 00:05:37,938
or natural hardware noise.

62
00:05:37,938 --> 00:05:40,416
Hardware resources are part of the result.

63
00:05:40,416 --> 00:05:44,174
The ordinary preparation used one native controlled Z gate.

64
00:05:44,174 --> 00:05:50,612
The original routed detector used six, and the equivalent bridge detector used four in the measured layouts.

65
00:05:50,612 --> 00:05:55,830
Reducing gate count is useful, but does not by itself prove better noisy fidelity.

66
00:05:55,830 --> 00:06:05,380
Across the initial experiment and the campaign, thirteen completed jobs executed 828 circuits and about one point sixteen million shots.

67
00:06:05,380 --> 00:06:12,032
Finalized records show 338 charged quantum-compute seconds from a 600-second allowance.

68
00:06:12,032 --> 00:06:16,067
Queue time and local preparation are additional wall-clock costs.

69
00:06:16,067 --> 00:06:19,605
The recorded remaining allowance is not a live account balance.

70
00:06:19,605 --> 00:06:28,992
Our recommendation is that quantum-cloud tools expose placement choices with repeated reference controls, raw counts, and explicit uncertainty.

71
00:06:28,992 --> 00:06:38,467
Students and smaller research groups often have limited compute budgets, so an attractive but ineffective protection method has a real opportunity cost.

72
00:06:38,467 --> 00:06:42,757
Reporting failures and discarded shots helps them make fair comparisons.

73
00:06:42,757 --> 00:06:48,808
More broadly, users should not treat a high reported fidelity as a guarantee for high-stakes decisions.

74
00:06:48,808 --> 00:06:56,860
A trustworthy report explains what it verified, which noise conditions it tested, and how much evidence or output it discarded.

75
00:06:56,860 --> 00:07:06,772
The repository includes the executed notebook, plots, raw counts, logical and native quantum instructions, and finalized usage records.

76
00:07:06,772 --> 00:07:12,259
Rebuilding the presentation reanalyzes saved evidence without submitting another quantum job.

77
00:07:12,259 --> 00:07:17,989
A portability fix resolves historical plan paths locally and still checks their recorded hash.

78
00:07:17,989 --> 00:07:20,392
We follow the general Track Three prompt.

79
00:07:20,392 --> 00:07:30,094
The optional geometry challenge asks for three specified architectures and nine core cases; those complete common definitions were not supplied, and this

80
00:07:30,094 --> 00:07:33,236
project does not claim to complete that comparison.

81
00:07:33,236 --> 00:07:37,052
This boundary keeps our conclusion consistent with the evidence.

82
00:07:37,052 --> 00:07:46,566
Our answer is that a noisy quantum result can become more trustworthy when we verify a known target, repeat the comparison on new data, and report the

83
00:07:46,566 --> 00:07:47,147
cost.

84
00:07:47,147 --> 00:07:49,940
Placement improved our recorded confirmations.

85
00:07:49,940 --> 00:07:54,834
Error detection helped a specified X-error mixture while discarding samples.

86
00:07:54,834 --> 00:07:56,677
The limits are part of the result.

87
00:07:56,677 --> 00:07:57,930
Thank you for watching.
